---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import ProjectGrid from '../../components/ProjectGrid.astro';
import { archive, getArchive } from '../../lib/collections';
import { pageSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const entries = await getArchive();
const files = entries.reduce((n, entry) => n + entry.data.archive!.files.length, 0);

const sections = archive.categories.map((category) => ({
  ...category,
  entries: entries.filter((entry) => entry.data.archive!.category === category.id),
}));

---

<Base
  title={archive.seoTitle}
  description={archive.description}
  schema={pageSchema(archive.seoTitle, archive.href, archive.description, 'CollectionPage')}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 sm:py-28" data-discipline="design">
    <a
      href="/work#design"
      class="group inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-(--color-faint) transition-colors hover:text-(--color-fg)"
    >
      <span
        class="transition-transform duration-300 ease-(--ease-out-expo) group-hover:-translate-x-1"
        aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span
      >
      Design
    </a>

    <header class="mt-8 mb-16 max-w-2xl" data-intro>
      <p class="accent-text mb-4 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.16em]">
        Design · Historical archive
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl">{archive.seoTitle}</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-(--color-muted)">
        <em>{archive.subtitle}</em>
      </p>
      <div class="prose mt-8 sm:text-justify sm:hyphens-auto">
        <p>
          Before the qualifications, professional design work, marketing campaigns and game development, there was DeviantArt.
        </p>
        <p>
          Growing up with undiagnosed autism and ADHD wasn't always easy. I often struggled with things that seemed to come naturally to other people, and those difficulties weren't always understood.
        </p>
        <p>
          I was frequently told that I was intelligent but didn't apply myself, that I was slow, or that I wouldn't amount to much if I didn't try harder. What people didn't necessarily see was that I was trying. I just didn't always understand why my mind seemed to work differently.
        </p>
        <p>
          Creativity became my outlet.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was somewhere I could experiment, explore ideas and express myself without needing to fit into someone else's expectations. Whether I was working with Photoshop, creating digital artwork, experimenting with website layouts or making graphics for online communities, I could lose myself in the process of creating something.
        </p>
        <p>
          I remember thinking to myself:
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>"When I find that one thing I'm good at, you'll all see."</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Looking back, I recognise that there was a lot of frustration behind those words. I wanted to prove that I was capable of something, that I wasn't defined by the things I struggled with or by what other people thought my future would look like.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the name Neo Android, I spent years creating artwork inspired by anime, video games, fantasy, music and whatever else caught my imagination.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some of it is wonderfully dated now. The dramatic lighting, excessive textures, elaborate typography and an almost criminal enthusiasm for Photoshop blending modes are very much products of their time.
        </p>
        <p>
          But I wouldn't change them.
        </p>
        <p>
          These pieces represent a period when I was discovering what I enjoyed, developing skills through experimentation and finding confidence in my own creativity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Today, I've gone on to study graphic design, graduate with First Class Honours in Digital Marketing, work professionally in creative and digital roles, and pursue a master's degree in Games and Extended Reality.
        </p>
        <p>
          But the most interesting thing about looking back at this work isn't how much I've achieved since.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's realising that I didn't need to wait until I found something I was good at. I was already learning, experimenting and creating all along.
        </p>
        <p>
          I just couldn't see it yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          This archive is a little tribute to that younger version of me, to the online communities that encouraged creativity, and to the beginning of a journey I'm still figuring out.
        </p>
        <p>
          And perhaps a reminder that sometimes the things we struggle with don't tell the whole story of what we're capable of becoming.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <aside
      class="glass mb-12 max-w-3xl rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-(--color-muted)"
      aria-label="Archive note"
      data-reveal
    >
      {archive.notice} Every piece is shown at its original size and proportions;
      {' '}{files} recovered files across {entries.length} entries.
    </aside>

    <nav aria-label="Archive categories" class="mb-20" data-reveal>
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm">
        {
          sections.map((section) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={`#${section.id}`}
                class="block rounded-full border border-white/12 bg-white/4 px-3.5 py-1.5 text-(--color-muted) transition-colors hover:bg-white/8 hover:text-(--color-fg)"
              >
                {section.label} <span class="text-(--color-faint)">({section.entries.length})</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-24">
      {
        sections.map((section) => (
          <section id={section.id} aria-labelledby={`${section.id}-heading`} class="scroll-mt-24">
            <SectionHeading
              id={`${section.id}-heading`}
              eyebrow={archive.title}
              title={section.label}
              blurb={section.blurb}
              discipline="design"
            />
            <ProjectGrid entries={section.entries} />
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <section class="mt-28 max-w-2xl" aria-labelledby="looking-back" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="looking-back" class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Looking Back, Moving Forward</h2>
      <div class="prose mt-8 sm:text-justify sm:hyphens-auto">
        <p>It's strange revisiting artwork I made so long ago.</p>
        <p>
          At the time, I wasn't thinking about career paths, design principles or professional
          portfolios. I simply enjoyed making things, experimenting with Photoshop and sharing my
          work with people who enjoyed similar interests.
        </p>
        <p>
          My techniques and understanding of design have changed considerably since then, but many
          of the things that inspired me haven't.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fantasy worlds, science fiction, characters, dramatic visuals and storytelling are still a
          huge part of what I love creating.
        </p>
        <p>
          These days, I'm exploring those interests through game development, Unreal Engine, Blender
          and extended reality.
        </p>
        <p>
          In some ways, it feels less like starting something new and more like returning to where
          it all began.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="/work#games"
          class="glass rounded-full px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium transition-[transform,background-color] duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:bg-white/10"
        >
          See my games &amp; XR work &rarr;
        </a>
        <a
          href="/work#design"
          class="rounded-full border border-white/12 px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-(--color-muted) transition-[transform,border-color] duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-white/25 hover:text-(--color-fg)"
        >
          Current design work &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
